Exactly—and now take the Hammer to control itself.

Control isn’t:

\text{make everything obey}

It’s:

\boxed{\text{identify where intervention can actually change the transition}}

So Control what you can control becomes a routing algorithm:

World
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\textbf{Controllable} &\rightarrow ACT\\
\textbf{Influenceable} &\rightarrow shape probabilities\\
\textbf{Observable} &\rightarrow TRACE / learn\\
\textbf{Uncontrollable} &\rightarrow adapt around it\\
\textbf{Unknown} &\rightarrow Y? / Survey Corps
\end{cases}

And there’s a flank: uncontrollable doesn’t mean useless.

Weather can’t be commanded, but it can be measured and navigated.

Another person’s decision can’t be controlled, but your offer, communication, boundaries, and response can.

Randomness can’t be commanded, but its distribution can sometimes be exploited.

The past can’t be changed, but its representation and what capability we extract from it can.

So the stronger doctrine is:

Control the controllable. Influence without confusing influence for control. Learn from the uncontrollable. Adapt to constraints. Search the unknown for new handles.

And then Kintsugi adds the feedback:

\text{ACT}
\rightarrow
\text{Outcome}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{update controllability map}

Meaning controllability itself is learned.

Something we thought uncontrollable may contain a hidden handle.

Something we thought controllable may turn out to have been coincidence.

That’s Survey Corps territory.

And ZPC prevents the worst mistake: assuming the current set of controls is the complete set of possible controls.

\boxed{
\text{Known controls}\neq\text{all possible handles}
}

So perhaps the operational maxim becomes:

Control what you can. TRACE what you can’t. Survey what you don’t yet know how to.

Then ACT only where the lever is real.

That’s how force becomes leverage.